                <div class="comp-footnotes__note">
                    <a href="javascript:;" id="footnote1" class="comp-footnotes__note-num" aria-label="Go to inline footnote 1"><sup>1</sup></a>
                    <div class="comp-footnotes__content"><p>Thank you to Kartik Athreya, Thomas Lubik, Jessie Romero and Pierre-Daniel Sarte for assistance preparing these remarks.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="comp-footnotes__note">
                    <a href="javascript:;" id="footnote2" class="comp-footnotes__note-num" aria-label="Go to inline footnote 2"><sup>2</sup></a>
                    <div class="comp-footnotes__content"><p>For example, see R&uuml;diger Bachmann, Steffen  Elstner, and Eric R. Sims, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/mac.5.2.217">Uncertainty and Economic Activity:  Evidence from Business Survey Data</a>,&rdquo; <em>American Economic Journal: Macroeconomics</em>,  April 2013, vol. 5, no. 2, pp. 217-249; Christopher D. Carroll, &ldquo;<a href="https://academic.oup.com/qje/article/118/1/269/1917034">Macroeconomic Expectations of  Households and Professional Forecasters</a>,&rdquo; <em>Quarterly  Journal of Economics</em>, February 2003, vol. 118, no. 1, pp. 269-298; Ryan  Chahrour and Kyle Jurado, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/aer.20170792">News or Noise? The Missing Link</a>,&rdquo; <em>American Economic Review, </em>July 2018, vol. 108, no. 7, pp.  1702-1736; Thomas A. Lubik and Frank Schorfheide, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/3592775">Testing  for Indeterminacy: An Application to U.S. Monetary Policy</a>,&rdquo; <em>American Economic Review</em>, March 2004, vol. 94, no. 1, pp. 190-217; Pierre-Daniel  Sarte, &ldquo;<a href="https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/jmcb.12143">When is Sticky Information More  Information?</a>&rdquo; <em>Journal of Money, Credit, and Banking</em>, September  2014, vol. 46, no. 7, pp. 1345-1379.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="comp-footnotes__note">
                    <a href="javascript:;" id="footnote3" class="comp-footnotes__note-num" aria-label="Go to inline footnote 3"><sup>3</sup></a>
                    <div class="comp-footnotes__content"><p>B. Ravikumar, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.stlouisfed.org/on-the-economy/2018/april/stock-ownership-trended-past-few-decades">How Has Stock Ownership Trended in  the Past Few Decades?</a>&rdquo;  St. Louis Fed <em>On the Economy </em>blog,  April 9, 2018.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="comp-footnotes__note">
                    <a href="javascript:;" id="footnote4" class="comp-footnotes__note-num" aria-label="Go to inline footnote 4"><sup>4</sup></a>
                    <div class="comp-footnotes__content"><p>Santiago Pinto, Robert Sharp, and  Pierre-Daniel Sarte. &ldquo;The Information Content and Statistical Properties of  Diffusion Indices,&rdquo; conditionally accepted at the <em>International Journal of Central Banking</em>, 2018. Working paper  version <a href="/publications/research/working_papers/2015/wp_15-09">here</a>.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="comp-footnotes__note">
                    <a href="javascript:;" id="footnote5" class="comp-footnotes__note-num" aria-label="Go to inline footnote 5"><sup>5</sup></a>
                    <div class="comp-footnotes__content"><p>While firms focus  strongly on GDP expectations, research has noted that inflation expectations  actually move <em>less</em> than one would  expect in a world where businesses and consumers are so well-informed. That&rsquo;s  because, for most firms, their industry pricing power trumps aggregate  statistics in importance. See, for example, Olivier Coibion, Yuriy  Gorodnichenko, and Saten Kumar, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/aer.20151299">How Do Firms Form  Their Expectations? New Survey Evidence</a>,&rdquo; <em>American  Economic Review</em>, September 2018, vol. 108, no. 9, pp. 2671-2713; and Gregory  N. Mankiw and Ricardo Reis, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/000282806777211937">Pervasive  Stickiness</a>,&rdquo; <em>American Economic Review</em>, May 2006, vol.  96, no. 2, pp. 164-169.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="comp-footnotes__note">
                    <a href="javascript:;" id="footnote6" class="comp-footnotes__note-num" aria-label="Go to inline footnote 6"><sup>6</sup></a>
                    <div class="comp-footnotes__content"><p>Coibion, Gorodnichenko, and Kumar  (2018).</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="comp-footnotes__note">
                    <a href="javascript:;" id="footnote7" class="comp-footnotes__note-num" aria-label="Go to inline footnote 7"><sup>7</sup></a>
                    <div class="comp-footnotes__content"><p>William T. Gavin and Rachel J.  Mandal, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.stlouisfed.org/publications/regional-economist/july-2000/inside-the-briefcase-the-art-of-predicting-the-federal-reserve">Inside the Briefcase: the Art of  Predicting the Federal Reserve</a>,&rdquo;  Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis <em>Regional  Economist</em>, July 2000.</p></div>
                </div>
                <div class="comp-footnotes__note">
                    <a href="javascript:;" id="footnote8" class="comp-footnotes__note-num" aria-label="Go to inline footnote 8"><sup>8</sup></a>
                    <div class="comp-footnotes__content"><p>Richard H. Clarida, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/clarida20190409a.htm">The Federal Reserve's Review of Its  Monetary Policy Strategy, Tools, and Communication Practices</a>,&rdquo; Speech at the &ldquo;Fed Listens: Distributional  Consequences of the Cycle and Monetary Policy&rdquo; conference, Federal Reserve Bank  of Minneapolis, Minneapolis, Minnesota, April 9, 2019.</p></div>
